---
import { GITHUB_URL } from '@lib/site';
---

<section class="border-t border-line bg-app" aria-labelledby="why-title">
  <div class="wrap py-16 md:py-24 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 lg:gap-12">
    <div class="lg:col-span-5">
      <h2 id="why-title" class="h2">
        Other trackers have MCP too.
      </h2>
      <p class="prose mt-5 max-w-[46ch]">
        Agent access alone is no longer a difference. The case for this one rests on how much of the product the agent
        reaches, and how plainly it reads.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="lg:col-span-6 lg:col-start-7 rule-list">
      <div class="py-6">
        <h3 class="h3">No side door.</h3>
        <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
          The MCP server is a client of the public REST API. If the app can do something, the agent can, credentials
          aside. If the app cannot, neither can the agent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="py-6">
        <h3 class="h3">Names, not numbers.</h3>
        <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
          <code>ACME-12</code>, <code>@alex</code>, <code>In Progress</code>, <code>My open bugs</code>. Markdown in,
          Markdown out. Nothing to look up before a model can reason about the work.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="py-6">
        <h3 class="h3">One query language.</h3>
        <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
          The agent searches with the same language as the search bar, saved views and report scopes, so a query it
          writes is one you can read and reuse.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="py-6">
        <h3 class="h3">Open code.</h3>
        <p class="prose mt-2 text-base">
          AGPL-3.0. Read every tool definition, or run the same build yourself.
          <a class="link" href={GITHUB_URL}>
            Source on GitHub
            <span class="arr ext" aria-hidden="true">
              ↗
            </span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
